unclassic — a universal unarchiver for Classic Mac OS formats
Licensed under LGPL-2.1-or-later (see LICENSE).

This project's decoders are transcriptions/ports of algorithms from:

  * XADMaster — the engine of The Unarchiver, by Dag Ågren (MacPaw).
    https://github.com/MacPaw/XADMaster — LGPL-2.1.
    StuffIt (classic + SIT5 + Arsenic), Compact Pro, PackIt, BinHex,
    MacBinary and related handles were used as the primary reference.
    Files that transcribe XADMaster code carry a
    "Source: reference/XADMaster/<file>" comment.

  * macutils 2.0b3, by Dik T. Winter et al. (descended from Allan G.
    Weber's unsit and D. J. Perry's unstuffit) — late-80s C. Its README
    states that every algorithm in it is implemented from publicly
    available software, that use and redistribution are explicitly
    allowed, and that "for some parts of it the GNU software license
    does apply"; the files here that cite it say "LGPL, see NOTICE",
    and this is what that points at.
    Used as a secondary reference for the oldest formats, and as the
    ONLY reference for StuffIt method 6, "fixed Huffman", which
    XADMaster recognizes but does not implement: `src/fmt_sit.c` and
    the generated `src/sit6_tables.h` transcribe `macunpack/sit.c`.
    A build of `macunpack` is also the second oracle for the
    differential test suite (TESTING.md) — test-time only, not linked
    into anything shipped.

  * Alysis SuperDisk! / Disk Expander (Alysis Software Corporation,
    1991-1992). No public specification of this format exists and no
    other implementation of it does either, so `src/fmt_superdisk.c`
    was written from the format's own 68k decoders, read out of the two
    self-extracting archives in the test corpus: the container walk and
    methods 1-9 from the self-extractor's `CODE 2`, method 11 from the
    "SuperDisk! Add-In Module" one of them carries, and the method
    table from the `Cmpr` resources of the Disk Expander extension that
    method 11 unpacks. No code was copied; every routine cited in that
    file is an address in a binary this project does not redistribute.
    The archives themselves are fetched by URL, never committed
    (fixtures/SOURCES.md).

  * The cryptographic primitives five of the encryption schemes need —
    DES, MD5, RC4, SHA-1 and AES — are transcribed from XADMaster's
    own `Crypto/` directory (des.c, md5.c, sha.c, hmac_sha1.c, the
    Gladman AES tables) and from the handles that use them; the tables
    are precomputed by `tools/gen_crypt_tables.py` because this engine
    may not have global mutable state, and the generator checks the AES
    S box against its own definition rather than trusting the copy.
    Each is graded in `tests/unit_formats.c` against an implementation
    that is not ours — python's hashlib and hmac, and `openssl enc`.

  * Compact Pro (Bill Goodman, 1990-1995). Its ENCRYPTION is the one
    scheme in this project that no reference implementation reads and
    no publication describes; The Unarchiver's own format notes say
    "the algorithm for this is unknown". `src/unc_crypt.c` implements
    it from the format's own 68k code, read out of Compact Pro 1.51's
    `CODE` 3 — the key derivation, the 64-bit CRC table, the seed and
    both the encrypt and decrypt loops, each cited by address in that
    file. No code was copied; every routine named there is an offset in
    a binary this project does not redistribute (the container and its
    two compression methods are transcribed from XADMaster and
    macutils, as above).

  * btoa 5.2.1, by Paul Rutter, Joe Orost and Stefan Parmark (1996) —
    and this one is a REFERENCE ONLY, deliberately, because its terms
    are not ours. Its README says btoa "is in the public domain. You
    may use it, give it away, and make improvements, as long as the
    names of the developers are mentioned and you don't use it to earn
    money. It may NOT be used commercially without my permission."
    That is a non-commercial restriction, and Debian shipped it in
    `non-free` for exactly that reason; it is incompatible with this
    project's LGPL grant, under which somebody may charge for what they
    do with unclassic.

    So `src/fmt_btoa.c` implements the FORMAT — the two headers, the
    base-85 alphabet, the `z` and `y` escapes, the three checksum
    recurrences, the per-line check and the padding rule — and is not a
    copy of `atob.c`'s code: no function, buffer or control structure is
    carried over, and the file says which routines were read for which
    fact. Format facts are not the developers' to license; their
    implementation is, and it is not here. `reference/btoa` is fetched
    by `tools/fetch_references.sh`, never committed, never linked into
    anything, and used as a test-time oracle (`tools/run_btoa.py`
    compiles it and decodes what it encodes). The names are mentioned,
    here and in the source. DECISIONS.md 2026-08-23 (btoa's licence).

  * LHa for UNIX 1.14i (Yoshizaki, Tagawa, Oki, Watazaki, Okamoto,
    1989-2000) and lhasa 0.4.0 (Simon Howard, ISC licence) — both
    REFERENCES ONLY for `src/fmt_lzh.c` and `src/unc_lzah.c`, which are
    transcribed from XADMaster's LZH handles as above. LHa is read as
    the original statement of the header levels and of LArc's two
    methods, and lhasa as an independent reading of both; the file
    comments say which fact came from which, and three of them were
    settled by neither but by an archive (DECISIONS.md 2026-08-23,
    LZH). Neither is committed — `tools/fetch_references.sh` fetches
    them — and neither is linked into anything shipped: LHa is
    compiled at test time as an ENCODER and lhasa as a second decoder
    (`tools/run_lzh.py`). lhasa's ~160 test archives are the specimens
    that put the format in scope; seven are fixtures, fetched by URL
    and never committed, like every other fixture
    (fixtures/SOURCES.md).

  * "Stuffit Method 15 compression format" by Matthew T. Russotto —
    the only public specification of the StuffIt 5 "Arsenic"
    (BWT + MTF + arithmetic coding) method.
    http://www.russotto.net/arseniccomp.html

DATA THAT IS NOT OURS AND IS SHIPPED ANYWAY

  * `src/ss_dict.h` contains 9,679 bytes that are
    **Copyright (c) 1993 Aladdin Systems, Inc.** — the `PrAm` -4048
    resource of StuffIt SpaceSaver, and the four bytes of `PrAm` -4047
    beside it. They are not code and no algorithm was copied: they are
    the table SpaceSaver's LZSS starts its window with, so a compressed
    file cannot be decoded to the right bytes without exactly these.
    Everything else in this repository is either ours, LGPL, CC0, or
    fetched rather than committed. This is the exception, it is marked
    as one, and it is not covered by this project's LGPL grant.

The `DDA2` entry that carries no file header of its own — DiskDoubler
Pro's form for a file it could not compress — and the whole of the
`SPLT` split container (src/fmt_ddseg.c), neither of which any other
reader implements, were derived from the specimens in Stephan Sokolow's
`ssokolow/diskdoubler-test-files`, released under CC0 and published
beside the original files they were made from. No code was involved:
the layout is read off the records, and the originals are what grade
the result (tools/run_ddtf.py). Like every other archive here, those
files are fetched, never committed.

